# TODO Markdown Style

- Do not hard-wrap normal TODO prose just to satisfy an arbitrary column width.
- Keep paragraphs, checklist items, and simple bullets on single logical lines when practical.
- Use extra line breaks only where structure benefits from them, such as headings, admonitions, tables, code fences, and separate list items.
- If a TODO line becomes awkwardly long, shorten or rewrite it instead of inserting arbitrary wraps.
- Use Markdown tables only for compact data with short cells.
- If a table would become wide or require sentence-length cells, replace it with bullets or short subsections instead.
- When a compact table is appropriate, keep columns vertically aligned and re-align the full table whenever rows are added or edited.
- Prefer lean, execution-focused wording over large narrative prompt blocks.

## Example

```markdown
> **Goal:** Keep this paragraph as one logical line unless structure truly needs a break.

| Column A | Column B |
| -------- | -------- |
| Value    | Value    |

- [ ] Keep this checklist item on one line when practical.
- `.cursor/rules/example.mdc`
  Scope: always-on.
  Covers: use a bullet block instead of a wide planning table.
```
